proxy_service: the offline login answers only the account's credential

handle_offline_login gave the cached login2 body -- the account's session
token included -- to any login or login2 naming an account the store held,
whenever upstream could not be reached. It compared no password and no
token, checked no Host and no Origin, and did not ask whether the account
was the one this device is configured for; a process on the device, or a
page reaching the loopback port by DNS rebinding while the link was down,
could take the token by naming the account (pixelelated audit #666 PL-01,
F-01/F-02; reproduced on a ROCKNIX image with the link cut).

The offline answer is now given only to a request that presents the
configured account's credential: t= equal to the cached row's token, or
p= whose salted hash (PBKDF2-SHA256, 20000 rounds, a random 16-byte salt
per recorded hash) matches the one recorded beside that row at the last
successful online login2 that carried a password. The account must be the
one the device's settings name (the sources resolve_credentials reads),
the Host a loopback name and no Origin sent. Anything else is told what
RetroAchievements tells a wrong password -- 401, Success false,
invalid_credentials -- with no token and no X-RA-Offline. A login that
failed upstream records nothing.

The hash lives in one new api_cache column, credentialHash, added by the
same ALTER pattern as sourceRomPath and never rewritten. A row from before
it has none: RetroArch holds the token only in memory and signs in with
the password at every start, so until the next online login records a
hash, the configured account's password is answered unverified, as
before, and the service's log says so once (read both, write the new
one). Nothing is migrated or purged. The three upstream
tests of the offline login now name the account, record the password and
ask by a loopback Host, as a client on the device does.

Written for ROCKNIX (fork issue pixelelated#666, bug pixelelated#1157).
Offered upstream (D-WORKFLOW-164, the #585 queue); not sent
(D-WORKFLOW-165).

